 ## Overview
-Adaptive Dual Contouring extracts a manifold mesh from a scalar density function by building an adaptive octree, solving Quadratic Error Functions (QEF) at nodes to determine vertex positions, and generating a mesh from the resulting constrained vertices [S1, S2, S3, unofficial]. It solves a Quadratic Error Function (QEF) at each node to determine vertex positions, then recursively collapses internal octree nodes into 'pseudo-leaf' nodes if the resulting QEF error is below a threshold, preserving sharp features defined by the sign configuration of child nodes [S1, S3, unofficial]. Unlike standard Dual Contouring which places one vertex per leaf voxel, this method reduces vertex count by merging internal nodes, producing a coarser but topologically consistent mesh suitable for real-time rendering or further processing [S2, unofficial].
+Adaptive Dual Contouring extracts a manifold mesh from a scalar density function by building an adaptive octree, solving Quadratic Error Functions (QEF) at nodes to determine vertex positions, and generating a mesh from the resulting constrained vertices [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. It recursively collapses internal octree nodes into 'pseudo-leaf' nodes if the resulting QEF error is below a threshold, preserving sharp features defined by the sign configuration of child nodes [S1, unofficial][S3, unofficial]. Unlike standard Dual Contouring which places one vertex per leaf voxel, this method reduces vertex count by merging internal nodes, producing a coarser but topologically consistent mesh suitable for real-time rendering or further processing [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
